dnf-automatic-reboot
====================

Unattended update + conditional reboot for EL8 and EL9 (RHEL, Oracle Linux).
Companion to dnf-automatic.  Handles the cases dnf-automatic does not:

  - Refreshes the repository metadata before each update, so dnf-automatic
    sees today's advisories rather than the cache's.
  - Runs dnf-automatic under a systemd shutdown inhibitor, so an accidental
    reboot does not corrupt a partial update; when logind refuses the lock,
    no update runs and the run fails.
  - Filters false-positive reboot triggers on positive evidence only: the
    running kernel already being the newest installed one, or matching ELF
    build-ids between a running daemon and its on-disk binary.  Anything
    that cannot be verified either way still triggers a reboot.
  - Detects genuine reboots and schedules a timed reboot.  While the GRUB
    default would not boot the newest kernel, it requests no reboot at all
    and fails each run with the repair.
  - Restarts services still mapping pre-update files, which
    needs-restarting -r never reports.
  - An independent watchdog timer stops a run whose processes have read
    and written nothing for too long, and leaves a working run alone
    however long it takes.  It never reboots; see "Fixed limits".
  - Names, by PID and cgroup, any dnf command already running and warns
    logged-in users; dnf-makecache.service, the distribution's metadata
    timer, is left out.  Arguments are never shown: they can carry a
    password.
  - Refuses to run while /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf has an error; see
    "Configuration".


Files installed
---------------

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/run.sh
      Main orchestration.  Called by the service unit.

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/watchdog.sh
      Independent watchdog.  Called every 5 minutes by watchdog.timer.

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/needs-reboot.sh
      Isolated reboot-decision logic.  Called by both run.sh and watchdog.sh.
      Handles false-positive filtering.
      Exit codes: 0=no reboot needed, 1=reboot needed,
                  2=undecidable: an invalid configuration, a tool error, or
                    a kernel that needs an operator (no reboot for any
                    package; the run is marked failed).

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/notify-failure.sh
      Failure notifier.  Started by OnFailure= from both service units and
      from the scheduled reboot; writes the reason to wall(1), the journal
      and the log.

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/cancel-reboot.sh
      Cancels a pending reboot and allows update runs again.  See
      "Scheduled reboots".

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/reboot-if-pending.sh
      The command of the scheduled reboot; reboots only while one is
      pending.  Not for interactive use.

  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/check-config.sh
      Validates /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf and names every error, or
      every value that differs from the shipped configuration.  Run by the
      RPM after every install and upgrade, and by hand.  Does not edit the
      file.  See "Configuration".

  /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf
      Configuration; each setting is explained in the file.  An edited copy
      is kept across upgrades (%config(noreplace)), with the new one beside
      it as .rpmnew.

  /usr/share/dnf-automatic-reboot/automatic-reboot.conf
      The configuration as shipped, which check-config.sh compares with.

  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dnf-automatic-reboot.service
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dnf-automatic-reboot.timer
      Main service + timer (default: daily at 03:00 UTC, plus up to
      10 min random delay).

  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dnf-automatic-watchdog.service
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dnf-automatic-watchdog.timer
      Watchdog service + 5-minute polling timer.

  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dnf-automatic-reboot-notify@.service
      Instantiated by OnFailure= with the failed unit name, so a failed run
      is never visible only to somebody who thinks to read the journal.

  /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/dnf-automatic-reboot.conf
      Creates the log file and state directory with the intended mode and
      SELinux label before any script writes to them.

  /etc/logrotate.d/dnf-automatic-reboot
      Weekly rotation, 8 kept, compressed.

  /etc/dnf/plugins/needs-restarting.d/automatic-reboot.conf
      Adds audit to the packages needs-restarting -r checks, one package
      name per line.

  /var/log/dnf-automatic-reboot.log
      All events logged here in addition to the journal.

  /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/restart-state
      Restart-state learning: tracks, per package, whether it has been
      confirmed as a false positive after surviving a real reboot still
      flagged at the same version.  See "Restart-state learning" below.

  /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/kernel-reboot-attempts
      Consecutive reboots scheduled for a kernel version that has not become
      the running one.  Cleared by the first check after it does: the next
      run, or needs-reboot.sh run by hand.  See "Kernel reboot loop guard"
      below.


Quick start
-----------

Steps 1 and 2 must be completed before running dnf install.  The RPM
pre-install scriptlet validates them, and the GRUB settings in "UEK kernel
default" below, and refuses to install if they are not satisfied.

  # 1. Configure dnf-automatic: disable its own reboot handling and ensure
  #    updates are downloaded and applied.  upgrade_type may be default or
  #    security.  Put any comment on a line of its own: dnf-automatic reads
  #    "apply_updates = yes # x" as invalid.  The scriptlet and every run ask
  #    dnf-automatic's own config loader what it reads from the file.
  vi /etc/dnf/automatic.conf
      [commands]
      upgrade_type = default
      download_updates = yes
      apply_updates = yes
      reboot = never

  # 2. Disable the stock dnf-automatic timers.  This package provides its
  #    own schedule; running both would apply updates twice.
  systemctl disable --now dnf-automatic.timer dnf-automatic-install.timer 2>/dev/null || true

  # 3. Install the package
  dnf install dnf-automatic-reboot

  # 4. Ensure chrony steps the clock immediately on boot and signals sync.
  #    Critical on systems with no RTC (e.g. RPi4).
  grep -q 'makestep 1 -1' /etc/chrony.conf || echo 'makestep 1 -1' >> /etc/chrony.conf
  systemctl restart chronyd
  #    systemd-time-wait-sync.service is absent on UEK R8 and nothing else is
  #    ordered Before=time-sync.target, so the unit's Requires=time-sync.target
  #    gates nothing on its own.  chrony ships chrony-wait.service (chronyc
  #    waitsync), disabled by default; %post enables it.  Verify:
  systemctl is-enabled chrony-wait.service
  #    rtcsync is only meaningful with an RTC present; leave it commented out
  #    otherwise.

  # 5. Review and optionally tune behaviour, then validate the file
  vi /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf
  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/check-config.sh

  # 6. Enable this package's timers
  systemctl enable --now dnf-automatic-reboot.timer dnf-automatic-watchdog.timer

  # 7. Verify
  systemctl list-timers dnf-automatic-reboot.timer dnf-automatic-watchdog.timer


Checking inhibitor lock status
-------------------------------

  systemd-inhibit --list


Viewing logs
------------

  journalctl -u dnf-automatic-reboot.service
  journalctl -u dnf-automatic-watchdog.service
  tail -f /var/log/dnf-automatic-reboot.log


Configuration
-------------

  /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf explains each setting, its range and its
  default.  A line is a comment, a [section] header or key = value; a # after
  a blank starts a comment; yes/no settings accept yes, true, on, 1 and no,
  false, off, 0 in any case.  A key left out takes its default.

  Any other line, a key this version does not read, a key set twice, or a
  value outside its range is an error.  While the file has one, no update
  runs and no reboot is requested: run.sh fails at its start and
  needs-reboot.sh exits 2, each naming every error.  The failure notice goes
  out whatever the file says.  The watchdog reads no settings, so it keeps
  supervising a run in progress.

  # Validate, and list what differs from the shipped file
  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/check-config.sh

  An upgrade keeps an edited file and writes the new one beside it as
  /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf.rpmnew.  When the kept copy names a key the
  new version does not read, check-config.sh names the commands that keep a
  copy of yours and put the new one in place.  Carry your values over, then
  run it again.


Fixed limits
------------

  These are part of the design, not settings:

  - Reboot warning: a needed reboot happens 5 minutes after the run decides
    it; the wall message gives the time and the cancel command.
  - Blocked reboot: while a shutdown inhibitor blocks it, the reboot is
    retried every 30 seconds for 30 minutes, then reported as failed.
  - Kernel attempts: after 3 reboots that did not make the newest kernel the
    running one, no reboot happens until an operator repairs the boot.
  - Metadata: every run refreshes the repository metadata first.
  - Learning: every flagged package other than the kernel and systemd is
    learned (see "Restart-state learning").
  - chrony-wait.service is enabled at install.
  - Watchdog: a run is stopped when its processes have read and written
    nothing for 60 minutes before rpm starts installing, for 180 minutes
    while it installs or when dnf.log cannot tell, and for 60 minutes in
    its reboot check and service restarts.  A run that makes progress is
    never stopped, whatever its age.  The watchdog never reboots: after it
    stops a run in its reboot check, the next run decides the reboot.
  - A command that ignores SIGTERM at its timeout is killed 5 minutes later.


Why packages are flagged at all
--------------------------------

  needs-restarting -r runs exactly one test per package:

      rpm INSTALLTIME > boot time

  It never compares version strings.  The package list is fixed - kernel,
  kernel-core, kernel-rt, glibc, linux-firmware, systemd, dbus, dbus-broker,
  dbus-daemon, microcode_ctl - plus any *.conf file under
  /etc/dnf/plugins/needs-restarting.d/.  Oracle Linux 9's dnf-plugins-core
  adds kernel-uek and kernel-uek-core to the list itself.  This package adds
  audit, in /etc/dnf/plugins/needs-restarting.d/automatic-reboot.conf.

  The boot time comes from systemd's UnitsLoadStartTimestamp over D-Bus,
  falling back to max(mtime of /proc/1, btime in /proc/stat).  On a host with
  no RTC that timestamp is recorded before chrony corrects the clock, so it
  sits permanently in the past and every package on the list stays flagged
  forever.  Fitting an RTC removes the cause outright.

  # See the boot time it is deciding against
  busctl get-property org.freedesktop.systemd1 /org/freedesktop/systemd1 \
    org.freedesktop.systemd1.Manager UnitsLoadStartTimestamp \
    | awk '{print "unitsload", strftime("%F %T", $2/1000000)}'
  awk '/^btime/{print "btime    ", strftime("%F %T", $2)}' /proc/stat
  stat -c 'proc1     %y' /proc/1
  rpm -q --qf '%{INSTALLTIME:date}\n' glibc systemd


Unsigned repositories
----------------------

  Every run reports enabled repositories with an explicit gpgcheck=0 before
  installing anything, because unattended installation from an unsigned
  repository is a code path nobody is watching.  It reports and continues; it
  does not abort, since a deliberate local unsigned repository is a valid
  choice and refusing to patch the host is worse.

  # Same check by hand
  grep -rh gpgcheck /etc/yum.repos.d/ /etc/dnf/dnf.conf | sort | uniq -c


Repository metadata freshness
-----------------------------

  dnf-automatic installs from the metadata dnf has cached and fetches only
  what is older than metadata_expire (48 hours by dnf's default).
  dnf-makecache.timer, where enabled, refreshes it about every three hours.
  An advisory published after the last refresh waits for the next run,
  however often the run timer fires.

  Every run fetches current metadata (dnf makecache --refresh) before
  dnf-automatic starts, within metadata_refresh_timeout_seconds.  A fetch that
  fails or runs out of time is logged, the run continues from the cache as
  dnf-automatic alone would, and the completion line adds "(metadata
  refresh failed, cached metadata used)".

  dnf-makecache.timer is dnf's and is left alone.  After a run's refresh it
  fetches nothing until the metadata nears expiry, and it still serves
  interactive dnf, anything running dnf -C, and needs-reboot.sh run on its
  own.  On a slow link, raise metadata_refresh_timeout_seconds.

  # Which a run had, one line per repository
  grep -E 'has expired|using cache for' /var/log/dnf.log | tail -20


Advisories an update did not install
------------------------------------

  After each successful update, every security advisory that still applies
  to this host is reported at error level and to wall(1), with the packages
  it names: the update just run did not install it.  A repository priority=,
  an excludepkgs=, a versionlock or a dependency dnf cannot satisfy keeps it
  out while every run reports success and the host stays unpatched.

  Repository priority is the common cause.  From dnf.conf(5): "If there is
  more than one candidate package for a particular operation, the one from a
  repo with the lowest priority value is picked, possibly despite being a
  lower version."  A priority= on a distribution channel therefore pins every
  package that channel ships and masks errata from every other channel.
  Priorities protect a distribution from third-party repositories; between a
  distribution's own channels they only create this hole.  kernel-uek* and
  kernel* are different package names and never compete, so UEK needs none.

  # Diagnose a report
  dnf --disableexcludes=all updateinfo list --updates --security   # what applies
  dnf --assumeno --disableexcludes=all --setopt='*.priority=99' update --security

  # If levelling priorities surfaces the updates, remove priority= from the
  # distribution repositories.  Keep it only on third-party repos (EPEL).
  grep -rn 'priority' /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo

  # Turn the check off in /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf
  [security]
  warn_unapplied_advisories = no


Services still running pre-update code
---------------------------------------

  needs-restarting -r inspects only the fixed package list above.  A security
  update to sshd, nginx, bind or anything linking a refreshed library patches
  the on-disk file and leaves the vulnerable image resident, with no reboot
  flag raised.  run.sh closes this with a needs-restarting -s pass whenever no
  reboot is scheduled.

  # What is still mapping pre-update files right now
  LC_ALL=C dnf -q -C needs-restarting -s

  # Control it in /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf
  [services]
  restart_services                   = yes
  restart_services_excluded_patterns = postgresql.service,container-*.service

  Units are restarted with systemctl try-restart, so a unit that is not
  running is left alone.  dbus, dbus-broker, systemd-logind, auditd, user@,
  getty, serial-getty, autovt and this package's own units are never
  restarted; the setting adds units to that list and cannot remove one from
  it.  systemd refuses to restart auditd; it runs the updated version after
  the next reboot, which an update to the audit package requests.

  dbus, dbus-broker, systemd-logind and auditd have no session to wait for:
  when one of them still maps a file an update replaced, the host reboots,
  with the same warning and cancel command as for a kernel.  A getty or a
  user's session is replaced when it ends, and is only reported.
  restart_services = no turns this reboot off with the restarts.

  needs-restarting reads a process whose name holds a space, such as
  "spamd child", as started at boot, and flags its service at every run.  A
  flagged service is left running, and named in the run's summary, when none
  of its processes maps a replaced file and all of them started after the
  newest package install.


Kernel reboot loop guard
-------------------------

  needs-reboot.sh checks the kernel family that runs: the package owning
  /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/vmlinuz (kernel-uek-core, kernel-core) and its
  meta package (kernel-uek, kernel).  Before a reboot for a newer kernel of
  that family, it checks that grubby --default-kernel already points at it.
  If it does not, the reboot would come back up on the same kernel and be
  requested again on the next run - an unbounded reboot loop.

  A limit of 3 consecutive reboots that do not make the newest kernel the
  running one is the backstop for every other cause (GRUB fallback
  reverting saved_entry, a kernel that panics, a read-only /boot).

  A stale default, an exhausted limit, or a flagged kernel package of
  another family (RHCK updated on a UEK host) withholds every reboot, also
  one glibc or systemd needs.  Each run then fails, and its failure notice
  and the log name the kernel and the repair, until an operator acts.

  # Repair a stale GRUB default: point it at the newest installed kernel
  grubby --set-default "/boot/vmlinuz-$(rpm -ql kernel-uek-core \
      | sed -n 's#^/lib/modules/\([^/]*\)/vmlinuz$#\1#p' | sort -V | tail -1)"
  # and check that kernel updates advance it from now on
  grep GRUB_UPDATE_DEFAULT_KERNEL /etc/default/grub
  grep DEFAULTKERNEL /etc/sysconfig/kernel

  # After repairing a kernel that did not boot: inspect and reset the count
  column -t /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/kernel-reboot-attempts
  rm -f /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/kernel-reboot-attempts


Diagnosing the systemd false-positive
--------------------------------------

  # Identify which PIDs are triggering the systemd flag
  needs-restarting -v 2>/dev/null | grep -i systemd

  # Check which services use PrivateTmp (common cause)
  systemctl show '*' --property=PrivateTmp --property=Id 2>/dev/null \
      | paste - - | grep 'PrivateTmp=yes'

  # Compare running PID 1 build-id against on-disk binary
  eu-readelf -n /proc/1/exe              | grep 'Build ID'
  eu-readelf -n /usr/lib/systemd/systemd | grep 'Build ID'

  # If they match: false positive (PrivateTmp or debuginfo mismatch).
  # needs-reboot.sh handles this automatically via eu-readelf (elfutils, required).


Diagnosing kernel false-positive (aarch64 and x86_64)
------------------------------------------------------

  uname -r
  # The running family, and its installed releases, newest last
  rpm -qf "/lib/modules/$(uname -r)/vmlinuz" --qf '%{NAME}\n'
  rpm -ql kernel-uek-core | sed -n 's#^/lib/modules/\([^/]*\)/vmlinuz$#\1#p' | sort -V

  # Each release is the module directory name, which is what uname -r
  # prints for that kernel, so this is an equality test.  Equal means the
  # running kernel already is the newest installed one: confirmed false
  # positive, filtered automatically.  Different means a genuine update.


UEK kernel default (kernel not booted after update)
---------------------------------------------------

  Symptom: a new kernel-uek-core is installed but the system reboots into the
  previous kernel.  Cause: on OL9 UEK, kernel-install does not advance the GRUB
  saved_entry unless DEFAULTKERNEL and GRUB_UPDATE_DEFAULT_KERNEL are set, and
  neither is by default.

  The installer does not change either setting.  It refuses a host where
  kernel updates would not become the default, and names what to set:
      /etc/default/grub:      GRUB_UPDATE_DEFAULT_KERNEL=true
      /etc/sysconfig/kernel:  DEFAULTKERNEL=kernel-uek-core   (EL9; the running
                              kernel's package on any other host)
      grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-<newest installed kernel>
  Once set, kernel-install advances saved_entry on every future kernel update
  on its own.

  Check the settings:
      grep DEFAULTKERNEL /etc/sysconfig/kernel
      grep GRUB_UPDATE_DEFAULT_KERNEL /etc/default/grub
      grubby --default-kernel
      rpm -q --qf '%{VERSION}-%{RELEASE}.%{ARCH}\n' kernel-uek-core | sort -V | tail -1
      grub2-editenv /boot/grub2/grubenv list             # saved_entry


Restart-state learning
-----------------------

  needs-reboot.sh learns false positives for every flagged package other
  than the kernel and systemd (glibc is the common case) by watching whether
  it is still flagged by needs-restarting at the exact same version after a
  real reboot -- proven via /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id, not timestamps.
  The first time a package/version is flagged it still triggers one
  reboot; only reappearing at the same version after that reboot confirms
  it as a false positive and stops further reboots for it.  A new version
  on the same package always starts a fresh, unverified cycle.

  # View current restart-state (name, evr, boot_id, first_seen, state)
  column -t /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/restart-state

  # Force re-verification of a package (e.g. to discard a stale
  # confirmed entry after manually rebooting)
  sed -i '/^glibc\t/d' /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/restart-state


Scheduled reboots
-----------------

  A reboot runs from the transient timer
  dnf-automatic-reboot-scheduled-reboot.timer, 5 minutes after run.sh
  decides it.  The wall message and the log give the time.

  systemctl list-timers dnf-automatic-reboot-scheduled-reboot.timer
  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/cancel-reboot.sh             # cancel it

  From before a reboot is requested until the host reboots,
  /run/dnf-automatic-reboot.reboot-pending exists and
  dnf-automatic-reboot.service does not start; a timer start is skipped.
  run.sh also does not update while a scheduled reboot is waiting or under
  way.

  The reboot itself is reboot-if-pending.sh, run by the transient
  dnf-automatic-reboot-scheduled-reboot.service.  It reboots only while
  /run/dnf-automatic-reboot.reboot-pending exists, so once a cancellation
  removes the file, a timer that still fires does not reboot the host.

  cancel-reboot.sh removes the file and stops the timer.  It exits 1 and
  changes nothing while run.sh or reboot-if-pending.sh is working on a
  reboot (retry a minute later), once reboot-if-pending.sh has
  called systemctl reboot, while the host is shutting down, and when
  systemd's state cannot be read.  In the last two cases, reboot the host.

  The reboot respects shutdown inhibitors, such as the one a package
  transaction started by hand holds.  While one blocks it, while logind's
  inhibitors cannot be read, or after systemctl reboot fails with the host
  still up, the reboot is tried again every 30 seconds for 30 minutes, and
  can be cancelled in between; systemd-inhibit --list shows what holds it.
  The reboot is never forced.  Logged-in users do not hold it: they get the
  warning 5 minutes ahead, with the cancel command.

  If the reboot does not happen, dnf-automatic-reboot-notify@ reports the
  failed dnf-automatic-reboot-scheduled-reboot.service and says whether
  update runs are still held, and how to release them.

  When run.sh cannot establish whether its reboot request reached systemd,
  the file stays and the log says the outcome is unknown; a reboot that
  arrives late still happens.  Check the timer, then reboot or run
  cancel-reboot.sh.  A run the watchdog stops at that point leaves the same
  file; the watchdog reports it and fails when no reboot is waiting or
  under way.


Forcing a manual run
--------------------

  # Run a full update cycle now -- will apply updates and reboot if needed:
  systemctl start dnf-automatic-reboot.service
  journalctl -u dnf-automatic-reboot.service -f

  # If the service fails at startup, the journal names the reason: an
  # error in automatic-reboot.conf, a re-enabled stock timer, or a wrong
  # reboot or apply_updates setting in automatic.conf:
  journalctl -u dnf-automatic-reboot.service -e

  # Test needs-reboot detection only (no update, no reboot):
  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/needs-reboot.sh; echo "exit: $?"
  # Side effects, the same as in a scheduled run: restart-state records a
  # newly flagged package as pending, and confirms a pending one still
  # flagged after a reboot; for a pending kernel, kernel-reboot-attempts
  # counts the current boot as one attempt (once per boot).

  # Test watchdog (with no state file it exits immediately):
  /usr/libexec/dnf-automatic-reboot/watchdog.sh
  # While a run is in progress it acts as its timer would: it can stop a
  # run that has stalled, and never reboots.


Uninstalling
------------

  Installed from the RPM:

      dnf remove dnf-automatic-reboot

  This disables and stops both timers and removes every file the package
  owns: the scripts, units, tmpfiles.d and logrotate drop-ins, the log
  /var/log/dnf-automatic-reboot.log, and the state files restart-state and
  kernel-reboot-attempts.  An edited /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf or
  /etc/logrotate.d/dnf-automatic-reboot is kept with a .rpmsave suffix.
  Files the package does not own stay: rotated logs
  (dnf-automatic-reboot.log.1, .2.gz, ...) and
  /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot/.state.lock, which keeps its directory.
  Remove them with:
      rm -f /var/log/dnf-automatic-reboot.log.*
      rm -rf /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot

  Installed with make install:

      systemctl disable --now dnf-automatic-reboot.timer dnf-automatic-watchdog.timer
      make uninstall
      systemctl daemon-reload

  make uninstall removes the units, the drop-ins, the scripts, this
  documentation and /var/lib/dnf-automatic-reboot with its state.  It does
  not stop the timers, hence the first command, and it keeps
  /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf and the logs.  Remove them with:
      rm -f /etc/dnf/automatic-reboot.conf /var/log/dnf-automatic-reboot.log*


Hardware watchdog (recommended for headless systems)
-----------------------------------------------------

  For protection against kernel panics and OOM events that prevent any
  userspace recovery, enable the systemd hardware watchdog:

  /etc/systemd/system.conf:
      RuntimeWatchdogSec=60
      RebootWatchdogSec=10min

  This causes the kernel to reset the board if systemd stops petting the
  watchdog for 60 seconds -- covers cases this package cannot handle.
